<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tanagra</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Tanagra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Boeotian city famed for its painted terracotta figurines and the poet Corinna, and site of the battle where Sparta defeated Athens in 457 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tanagra lay in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asopus-boeotia">Asopus</a> valley, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>. Settled since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, it was a city by the Archaic period and a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League</a>, often second only to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tanagra was the home of the lyric poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinna">Corinna</a>, said to have competed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>. In 457 BCE <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> and its allies defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tanagra">Battle of Tanagra</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> won at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oenophyta">Oenophyta</a> soon afterwards and controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> for a decade. In the 4th century BCE Tanagra's workshops produced elegant terracotta figurines of women, now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanagra-figurine">Tanagras</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tanagra survived Theban dominance and the Macedonian period as a prosperous town and came under Roman control in 146 BCE. Its cemeteries, looted in the 1870s, filled museums with its figurines.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><pa